Ryujinx shader translator, Optimizer.RemoveNode: removing a node also takes it out of its sources' use lists, and when
a source is left without uses it goes on into the operation that defines that source. That operation is not removed from
its block, only from the use lists of its own sources; for an operation with side effects (a call, an atomic) whose result
went unused that is wrong: it stays in the program (IsUnused keeps it) but its sources now look unused, so the optimizer
removes their definitions while it still reads them, and the SPIR-V reads variables nothing assigns. GlobalToStorage
makes such calls: a RED (atomic without result) through a global address becomes "r = call ...; copy r" and the copy is
dead. Seen in OoT's GPU culling 4c1e8564 (AGENTS.md A17): the RED.E.MAX that writes the work-group count of each indirect
dispatch record got an undefined address high word and value, so every GPU-culled dispatch and mesh draw got x = 0. Now
the walk stops at operations with side effects. It also stops at the Ampere front end's cooperative-matrix HMMA
(ampere-coopmat-instruction.patch) while another of its destination registers is still used: that operation has one
destination per D register, and a dead copy of one of them took the whole multiply-add out of its sources' use lists,
so the loads of its A and B registers were removed (OoT's network pass f90d726d lost its weight loads). The Ryujinx code
in the context lines is MIT.
--- a/Translation/Optimizations/Optimizer.cs	2026-11-07 12:02:46.270009800 +0100
+++ b/Translation/Optimizations/Optimizer.cs	2026-11-07 12:02:46.356854300 +0100
@@ -381,7 +381,11 @@
                         continue;
                     }

-                    if (src.UseOps.Remove(node) && src.UseOps.Count == 0)
+                    // An operation with side effects (a call, an atomic) stays in its block when its result goes unused, so it keeps
+                    // its sources: going on into it here took it out of their use lists and let them be removed while it still reads
+                    // them (optimizer-remove-node-side-effects.patch). The same for a cooperative-matrix HMMA with another used result.
+                    if (src.UseOps.Remove(node) && src.UseOps.Count == 0 && !HasSideEffects(src.AsgOp) &&
+                        !(src.AsgOp is Operation mma && mma.Inst == Instruction.CooperativeMatrixMulAdd && !DestHasNoUses(mma)))
                     {
                         Debug.Assert(src.AsgOp != null);
                         nodes.Enqueue(src.AsgOp);
